1
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:13,851
MATTHEW: Easton, Pennsylvania. 1833. A man named Charles Getter is to be hanged for murder. The crowd that comes to watch is enormous. Estimates run from fifteen to twenty thousand people.

2
00:00:14,151 --> 00:00:24,590
MATTHEW: The execution is moved out of town to an island in the river. The first attempt fails. The second one doesn't. And the next year, the governor signs a law ending public executions in Pennsylvania.

3
00:00:24,890 --> 00:00:30,053
BENJAMIN QUILL: Quakers, hear ye, from the Palestra! A grim start, friends, but a humane end.

4
00:00:30,353 --> 00:00:34,635
GARY MERCER: Twenty thousand people to watch a hanging? What was wrong with these people?

5
00:00:34,935 --> 00:00:39,844
MATTHEW: Not enough schools, Gary. Which brings us to the governor. George Wolf. Pennsylvania's seventh.

6
00:00:40,144 --> 00:00:51,597
MATTHEW: George Wolf was born August 12th, 1777, in Allen Township, Northampton County, He studied at a local classical academy, read law, and was admitted to the bar in 1799.

7
00:00:51,897 --> 00:00:58,435
BENJAMIN QUILL: No Penn for Mister Wolf. A country academy and a stack of law books. Your humble servant approves.

8
00:00:58,735 --> 00:01:08,730
MATTHEW: He went to the United States House in 1824, a protectionist and a Jackson man. In 1829 he beat Joseph Ritner for governor, and in 1832 he beat Ritner again.

9
00:01:09,030 --> 00:01:15,610
MATTHEW: He served on the board of Lafayette College in Easton from 1827 until he died. He would make schools the fight of his life.

10
00:01:15,910 --> 00:01:24,201
MATTHEW: Now the count. Before 1790 Pennsylvania didn't have a governor. It had a Supreme Executive Council, and that council had a president. Seven of them.

11
00:01:24,501 --> 00:01:36,872
MATTHEW: The sixth president was Benjamin Franklin. The seventh was Thomas Mifflin, a 1760 graduate of the College of Philadelphia, which is Penn. Mifflin then became the first governor under the 1790 constitution.

12
00:01:37,172 --> 00:01:45,531
MATTHEW: Count from 1790, and it's Mifflin, McKean, Snyder, Findlay, Hiester, Shulze, and seven, George Wolf.

13
00:01:45,831 --> 00:01:52,039
BENJAMIN QUILL: And Franklin was the very first president of the Academy and College of Philadelphia. Penn's own. Hear ye.

14
00:01:52,339 --> 00:02:04,863
MATTHEW: Franklin is the story in miniature. He enslaved at least seven people over his life and ran ads selling enslaved people in his newspaper. In his last years he was president of the Pennsylvania Abolition Society. Both of those are Franklin.

15
00:02:05,163 --> 00:02:18,710
MATTHEW: Getter was hanged for the murder of a woman named Margaret Lawall. And the crowd that came to watch, up to twenty thousand people, was bigger than most towns in the state. Pennsylvania's leaders looked at that crowd and decided hanging had become a show. Wolf ended the show.

16
00:02:19,010 --> 00:02:24,243
BENJAMIN QUILL: A state that turns a hanging into a holiday has a schooling problem. Mister Wolf saw the connection.

17
00:02:24,543 --> 00:02:38,756
MATTHEW: Back to Wolf. Governors before him had tried to build a statewide system of common schools, open to every child, not just families who could pay. They failed. Wolf pushed, and in 1834 Pennsylvania passed its free school law.

18
00:02:39,056 --> 00:02:45,641
MATTHEW: He's remembered as the father of Pennsylvania's public schools. Easton put up a memorial gateway in his honor in 1888.

19
00:02:45,941 --> 00:02:52,124
MATTHEW: And on April 10th, 1834, he signed the law that ended public executions. Charles Getter was the last.

20
00:02:52,424 --> 00:02:56,450
GARY MERCER: Okay. Schools and no more public hangings. That's a good year.

21
00:02:56,750 --> 00:02:59,738
MATTHEW: It was a good spring, Gary. Then came August.

22
00:03:00,038 --> 00:03:16,780
MATTHEW: August 12th, 1834. Philadelphia. A mob of several hundred white men, many of them Irish immigrants, attacks a carousel on South Street called the Flying Horses, a place where Black and white residents mixed. Then it moves into Moyamensing and goes after Black-owned homes and buildings.

23
00:03:17,080 --> 00:03:34,545
MATTHEW: The mob grew to four or five hundred. Mayor John Swift gathered a posse of about three hundred. On the first night, eighteen men were arrested. By the end, about sixty. The committee that studied the damage put it at around four to six thousand dollars. Lucretia Mott, the Quaker abolitionist, wrote about it in her letters.

24
00:03:34,845 --> 00:03:38,253
GARY MERCER: Sixty arrests for forty-four buildings and two people dead.

25
00:03:38,553 --> 00:03:42,726
BENJAMIN QUILL: Philadelphia counted the windows, Gary. It was slower to count the people.

26
00:03:43,026 --> 00:03:53,748
MATTHEW: For three nights the mob attacks Black homes and churches. The African Presbyterian Church is targeted. The Wharton Street Church is destroyed. When it's over, forty-four buildings are gone and two people are dead.

27
00:03:54,048 --> 00:03:58,952
BENJAMIN QUILL: In the City of Brotherly Love, friends. The name was a promise, not a description.

28
00:03:59,252 --> 00:04:04,918
MATTHEW: The school law was a promise to every child in Pennsylvania. That August, the city showed who it actually protected.

29
00:04:05,218 --> 00:04:26,245
MATTHEW: And it got worse. Wolf lost in 1835 to Ritner when his own Democrats split. And in 1838 Pennsylvania wrote a new constitution that took the vote away from Black men. Robert Purvis led the protest, the Appeal of Forty Thousand Citizens Threatened with Disfranchisement. Black Pennsylvanians didn't get the vote back until 1870.

30
00:04:26,545 --> 00:04:38,153
MATTHEW: Wolf married Mary Erb in 1798. They had eight sons and a daughter. As governor he pushed the legislature to build canals, and to raise taxes to pay the state's debts for them. Taxes are never popular.

31
00:04:38,453 --> 00:04:49,210
MATTHEW: Ritner won that 1835 race as an Anti-Mason, and the Democrats split between Wolf and Henry Muhlenberg. The man who built the schools lost his job to a divided party and a split vote.

32
00:04:49,510 --> 00:04:57,578
MATTHEW: Andrew Jackson made Wolf First Comptroller of the Treasury, and later he ran the customs house in Philadelphia. He died there on March 11th, 1840.

33
00:04:57,878 --> 00:05:06,422
MATTHEW: There's a Wolf Street in South Philadelphia, schools in his name in Bath and Bethlehem, and a Wolf Hall at Penn State. Not Penn. Penn State.

34
00:05:06,722 --> 00:05:10,206
BENJAMIN QUILL: A distinction we at Penn insist upon. Quill out.

35
00:05:10,506 --> 00:05:14,894
MATTHEW: He opened the schoolhouse door. The rest of the state was still deciding who got through it.

36
00:05:15,194 --> 00:05:18,209
MATTHEW: So, Pennsylvania. Your lucky seven, one more time.

37
00:05:18,509 --> 00:05:24,761
MATTHEW: One. Born 1777 in Northampton County. Admitted to the bar in 1799.

38
00:05:25,061 --> 00:05:31,570
MATTHEW: Two. U.S. Congress 1824 to 1829. Elected governor in 1829 and 1832.

39
00:05:31,870 --> 00:05:36,195
MATTHEW: Three. Counting governors from 1790, George Wolf is Pennsylvania's 7th.

40
00:05:36,495 --> 00:05:39,831
MATTHEW: Four. Remembered as the father of Pennsylvania's public-school system.

41
00:05:40,131 --> 00:05:45,325
MATTHEW: Five. On April 10, 1834, he signed the law ending public executions.

42
00:05:45,625 --> 00:05:52,140
MATTHEW: Six. The 1834 Philadelphia race riot, while Wolf was governor. 44 buildings destroyed, 2 dead.

43
00:05:52,440 --> 00:05:56,002
MATTHEW: Seven. Wolf Street in Philadelphia and Wolf Hall at Penn State are named for him.

44
00:05:56,302 --> 00:06:07,942
MATTHEW: Seven states. Seven governors. Griswold, Fenner, Yates, Sullivan, Plumer, Dickerson, Wolf. Not one of them is famous. Every one of them made choices about who counted.

45
00:06:08,242 --> 00:06:15,035
GARY MERCER: Matthew, I came in knowing one governor from back east. Now I know seven, and I'm mad at about four of them.

46
00:06:15,335 --> 00:06:26,382
MATTHEW: That's the right number to be mad at, Gary. The Ivy League trained the men who ran these states. The question is never just who led. It's who got a say, who got left out, and who paid for it.

47
00:06:26,682 --> 00:06:29,221
MATTHEW: Lucky seven. Keep it mid. Keep it real.
